1: # Exhaustion of the actual affine and line lifts at additive caps
2: 
3: Proved as P3F.44 after the independent root audit. The local source,
4: resolved line and boundary marking are the actual ones constructed
5: in SC, OA and II. This comparison transports their boundary maps;
6: it does not identify different global annular gluings without
7: transporting those maps.
8: 
9: ## AE.1. The section group and its exact cyclic action
10: 
11: Let \(D\) be the canonical order-\(m\) root deck on the
12: constructed minimal regular source \(S'\to\Delta_z\). Its
13: action on an analytic section is
14: \[
15:  (D\cdot T)(z)=D\bigl(T(\zeta_m^{-1}z)\bigr).
16: \]
17: All sums below are sums in this actual section group. The marked
18: section \(Q'\) is \(D\)-invariant. The constructed branch
19: \(R\), with \(mR=Q'\), is also \(D\)-invariant. At a
20: reduced II, III or IV root it is the unique division branch
21: because the central identity group is additive and multiplication
22: by \(m\) is invertible on the component group. At a good root
23: it is the branch with central value \(O\).
24: 
25: Every affine source map with the specified linear deck is
26: \(a=t_S D\) for an actual analytic section \(S\). Its exact
27: norm equation is
28: \[
29:  a^m=t_{-nQ'},\qquad
30:  \sum_{j=0}^{m-1}D^jS=-nQ'.                         \tag{AE.1}
31: \]
32: Set \(Z=S+nR\). Then \(\sum D^jZ=0\). Conjugation of the
33: constructed source map \(a_0=t_{-nR}D\) by translation is
34: \[
35:  t_T a_0t_T^{-1}=t_{T-DT-nR}D.                    \tag{AE.2}
36: \]
37: Thus the precise required equation is \((1-D)T=Z\).
38: 
39: ## AE.2. An explicit solution on every constructed root
40: 
41: At a reduced root multiplication by \(m\) is a bijection on
42: germs of analytic sections. On the additive central identity
43: group its inverse is multiplication by \(1/m\), and on the
44: finite component group it is a bijection because \(m\) is
45: prime to its order. Each central inverse extends uniquely by
46: the analytic inverse function theorem. An analytic section in
47: its kernel has central value zero, so that same uniqueness
48: shows that it is the zero section. Define
49: \[
50:  T=-[m]^{-1}\left(\sum_{j=0}^{m-1}jD^jZ\right).
51:                                                                \tag{AE.3}
52: \]
53: Since \(D^m=1\), direct subtraction gives
54: \[
55:  (1-D)\sum_{j=0}^{m-1}jD^jZ
56:    =\sum_{j=0}^{m-1}D^jZ-mZ=-mZ.
57: \]
58: Equation (AE.3) therefore solves (AE.2) exactly. The translation
59: extends over the resolved minimal regular source by its actual
60: source group law and graph comparison, already used to extend
61: the norm map in SC/OA/II.
62: 
63: At a good root the central linear automorphism \(D_0\) has
64: order three, four or six and has no eigenvalue one. Hence
65: \(1-D_0\) is a surjective elliptic isogeny. Choose a central
66: point \(T_0\) with
67: \((1-D_0)T_0=Z_0\), and lift it to an analytic section
68: \(\widetilde T\) of the smooth elliptic family. Put
69: \[
70:  Z_1=Z-(1-D)\widetilde T.
71: \]
72: It has central value zero and still has zero cyclic norm.
73: The subgroup of analytic sections with central value zero has
74: unique \(m\)-division with central value zero, by the analytic
75: inverse function theorem at \(O\). Apply (AE.3) to \(Z_1\)
76: in this subgroup and call the result \(T_1\). Then
77: \(T=\widetilde T+T_1\) solves \((1-D)T=Z\). Substitution
78: in (AE.2) is the required actual source isomorphism. No choice
79: of a rational homology marking was used to obtain it.
80: 
81: This also verifies the good-root norm restriction without losing
82: the central point. On the good central fibre
83: \(1+D_0+\cdots+D_0^{m-1}=0\), so (AE.1) forces
84: \(nQ'_0=0\). For III/IV, \(Q'_0\) has order two/three
85: when its component is nonzero; coprimality with \(m\) excludes
86: that case. For II its actual order-six fixed-point calculation
87: gives \(Q'_0=O\), and the constructed norm is admissible.
88: 
89: ## AE.3. The actual translation lift on the specified line
90: 
91: Let \(N\) be the resolved root line already constructed. On
92: every reduced central component it has degree zero, and on a
93: good central fibre it has degree zero. Translation by \(T\)
94: preserves these degree vectors. On a smooth generic fibre,
95: \(t_T^*N\otimes N^{-1}\) is trivial: in a degree-zero
96: uniformization its factors of automorphy are constant in the
97: elliptic variable, so a lift of the translation leaves them
98: unchanged.
99: 
100: There is an extending holomorphic line isomorphism on the whole
101: source, not only a generic Picard-class equality. Apply proper
102: direct-image coherence to
103: \(L=t_T^*N\otimes N^{-1}\). Its direct image is torsion-free
104: of generic rank one on the disc, hence free after shrinking.
105: A nonzero generator evaluates to a section of \(L\) whose
106: divisor is vertical: on each generic compact elliptic fibre it
107: is a nonzero section of a trivial degree-zero line. Its degree
108: on each central component is zero. The exact component
109: intersection matrix consequently puts that divisor in its
110: primitive whole-fibre kernel. At these reduced or good roots
111: the fibre has multiplicity one, so dividing by its integral
112: base monomial removes the divisor. The resulting section is
113: nowhere zero and gives the required line map \(\widetilde t_T\).
114: 
115: This lift commutes with the actual suspension \(B_0\). On a
116: generic elliptic fibre write the degree-zero line as the quotient
117: of \(\mathbb C\times\mathbb C^*\) by constant factors of
118: automorphy. Source translations lift as
119: \((z,v)\mapsto(z+t,cv)\); these maps commute. The commutator
120: of the extending maps is a holomorphic unit on the root source.
121: It is one on every generic fibre and hence one everywhere.
122: Thus conjugating by \(\widetilde t_T\) transports the actual
123: line root while preserving \(B_0\), not merely its source
124: translation. The base monomial used above retains its deck
125: character in this transported map.
126: 
127: ## AE.4. The remaining finite delta character and the boundary map
128: 
129: After this source-and-line conjugation, compare the given line
130: root \(A\) with the constructed \(A_0\), which have the same
131: source map. Define the ratio by \(AA_0^{-1}=M_c\), where
132: \(M_c\) multiplies the line at base coordinate \(z\) by
133: \(c(z)\). This is a nowhere-zero holomorphic function
134: on \(S'\), hence a base unit \(c(z)\), because the source
135: fibres are compact and connected. The equality of their exact
136: norms is
137: \[
138:  \prod_{j=0}^{m-1}c(\zeta_m^jz)=1.                \tag{AE.4}
139: \]
140: Its central value is \(c(0)=\zeta_m^d\) for a retained
141: integer \(d\bmod m\). Divide this constant and take the
142: analytic logarithm \(h(z)\) with \(h(0)=0\). Equation
143: (AE.4) implies \(\sum D^jh=0\); the possible integral
144: logarithm constant is zero at the centre. The additive formula
145: \[
146:  b(z)=-\frac1m\sum_{j=0}^{m-1}jD^jh(z)
147: \]
148: gives \((1-D)b=h\), with \((Db)(z)=b(\zeta_m^{-1}z)\).
149: The complete conjugation identity, with its arrow fixed, is
150: \[
151:  A=M_{e^b}\,(\zeta_m^dA_0)\,M_{e^{-b}},
152: \]
153: because its scalar multiplier is
154: \(\zeta_m^d\exp(b(z)-b(\zeta_m^{-1}z))=c(z)\).
155: It leaves \(B_0\) unchanged
156: because that map fixes the base.
157: 
158: Consequently every actual affine line lift on each constructed
159: root is carried to \(\zeta_m^dA_0\) by the displayed maps,
160: with its finite delta character retained. Their restrictions to
161: the outer annulus are the boundary comparison maps. They may
162: contain a source translation and an integral line gauge; these
163: are transported, not set to zero. The one-cap topology proofs
164: allow the resulting integer collar coefficient in full, and
165: their degree-two matrix remains unimodular for every value.
166: Global parameter-return comparisons continue to use the actual
167: closing map and these transported characters, as in K24.
